{
  "teamId": 137,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 San Francisco Giants opened the year with a burst of optimism, racing out to a 14-7 April record that suggested another deep postseason run was within reach. The bullpen stumbled early — Camilo Doval surrendered a brutal two-run blast on Opening Weekend — but the offense answered right back, with Thairo Estrada and Austin Slater delivering immediate responses. Joc Pederson and Luis González kept the energy high late in April, and the Giants looked every bit like the 107-win club that had stunned the baseball world just a year before.\n\nMay brought turbulence and resilience in equal measure. The bullpen continued to leak runs through Tyler Rogers and Doval, but the lineup refused to quit. Joc Pederson authored a remarkable comeback against the Mets with a three-run home run and a clutch single, and Evan Longoria provided back-to-back walk-off moments against Colorado to close the month. The Giants finished May at 13-14, a losing record that masked how competitive they remained in nearly every game. The team's 3.85 ERA and deep rotation — led by Logan Webb — kept them in fights even when the pen could not hold leads.\n\nJune and July proved to be the season's undoing. A 13-13 June gave way to a punishing 11-17 July, as the bullpen hemorrhaged runs with alarming regularity. Mookie Betts torched Jarlín García for a three-run blast and Cody Bellinger crushed a grand slam off Sam Long during a brutal Dodgers series. The Giants fell out of playoff position and never truly recovered their footing. August began with more bullpen disasters — Brandon Drury raked Yunior Marte, Bryan Reynolds crushed John Brebbia — but two extraordinary moments kept the fire alive: Thairo Estrada's go-ahead two-run shot and Brandon Crawford's stunning walk-off blast on back-to-back mid-August days reminded everyone what this group was still capable of.\n\nSeptember became the Giants' last stand, and for a month, they played like contenders again. An 18-10 September surge — the best month of their season — was powered by younger voices like David Villar and the reliable Estrada, whose three-run homer against Colorado in mid-September captured the spirit of the stretch run. But the math was unforgiving. Too many losses had piled up in July and August, and despite finishing at a perfectly even 81-81, San Francisco missed the playoffs by the narrowest of margins. It was a season of flashes — brilliant ones — that never quite added up to something whole.",
  "chapters": [
